  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The text contains the paper through its experimental appendix; no apparent truncation or document mismatch.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T1",
    "tranche_reason": "It trains 2-layer MLPs on modular arithmetic tasks and reports train/test accuracy over training, directly testing grokking-like behavior in additional algorithmic settings.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports train/test accuracy curves and IPR measurements for trained networks, plus endpoint train/test accuracies for modular-polynomial tasks. The analytical fixed-weight solutions and constructed expert networks are not trained grokking results, so they are not included as rows.",
    "test_reason": "It trains 2-layer MLPs on modular addition and multiplication and reports train/test accuracy curves. The authors say these curves show grokking, and they track a continuous periodicity measure (IPR) during training. Both bear on V1 and V2 in the original algorithmic setting.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely right. The authors extend analytical MLP solutions to modular multiplication and many-term addition. They show that trained networks grok these tasks and learn similar periodic weights, and they build expert networks that solve arbitrary modular polynomials. They also hypothesize that only polynomials of the form h(g1(n1)+g2(n2)) mod p are learnable by training, citing Appendix C as evidence.",
    "recipe": "close",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses trained 2-layer MLPs and train/test accuracy on modular arithmetic, but focuses on multi-term addition, multiplication, and polynomial tasks; the main examples use MSE and the paper also tracks IPR.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper says the Appendix C results remain qualitatively unchanged upon changing or tuning hyperparameters, but gives no specific comparison values.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At endpoint, MLPs reach 100% test accuracy on polynomials of the form h(g1(n1)+g2(n2)) mod p. Small modifications give near-chance test accuracy (1.89–7.17%) or 72.32% despite 100% train accuracy. No training-time course is given.",
        "proposed_claim": "Which algorithmic tasks grok depends on whether the task has a separable h(g1(n1)+g2(n2)) structure; other tasks stay memorized.",
